**Read:** [Pressure regulator flow curves — Swagelok technical bulletin](../sources/Pressure-Regulator-Flow-Curves-Swagelok.lingua#r_4RBBVMRQSACN4TR38CDAMQ17K9), introductory figures and the chosen configuration's complete inlet-pressure and control-range curves.

Read the axis definitions before comparing curves: gauge or absolute pressure, gas species, reference volume-flow conditions, inlet pressure and configuration. A family of curves describes a set of conditions, not a universal relation with one curve valid for every gas or setting.

Identify the pressure change with increasing demand, the transition toward no flow, the behavior at large flow and any difference between increasing/decreasing demand. Then vary inlet pressure. A depleted cylinder can move the system into a different operating region before its total inventory is exhausted. Two stages may reduce one sensitivity while introducing intermediate storage and another set of limits.

Choose a required flow interval and an acceptable pressure interval, then test the whole operating trajectory against them. Include beginning and end of supply, startup, normal demand and a changed demand. Extrapolation beyond the measured range needs separate evidence.

**Work:** Annotate a manufacturer's plot without turning its illustrative curves into exact data. If digitizing, record pixel/axis uncertainty and source edition. Explain how a satisfactory steady curve could coexist with an unacceptable transient or impurity contribution from wetted surfaces.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
